# Security Hardening

## Secrets & Configuration

- Never hardcode API keys, tokens, or secrets as fallback defaults — use environment variables with fail-fast behavior
- Development-only fallbacks must be explicitly gated on a development mode check — never silently fall back
- All security-critical configuration must be validated at startup

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — hardcoded fallback
const apiKey = process.env.MY_API_KEY || 'sk-default-dev-key-12345';

// ✅ GOOD — fail fast
const apiKey = process.env.MY_API_KEY;
if (!apiKey) {
  throw new Error('MY_API_KEY environment variable is required');
}
```

## Electron Security

### BrowserWindow Configuration

All `BrowserWindow` instances must use secure defaults:

- `contextIsolation: true` — always enabled, never disabled
- `nodeIntegration: false` — always disabled for renderer windows
- `webSecurity: true` — always enabled
- `sandbox: true` — enable for any window that loads remote content
- Use `preload` scripts via `contextBridge` for any main-process APIs needed in the renderer

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
new BrowserWindow({
  webPreferences: {
    contextIsolation: false,
    nodeIntegration: true,
  }
});

// ✅ GOOD
new BrowserWindow({
  webPreferences: {
    contextIsolation: true,
    nodeIntegration: false,
    webSecurity: true,
    preload: path.join(__dirname, 'preload.js'),
  }
});
```

### IPC Safety

- All IPC channel names must be defined in `packages/shared/src/` as typed constants — never use raw string literals for channel names
- Validate all IPC inputs against shared type definitions — never trust data arriving from the renderer
- Never expose raw `error.stack` or internal file paths over IPC channels
- Use `getIpcErrorMessage()` to extract safe error messages from `super-ipc` wrapped errors (see `ipc-error-handling.mdc`)

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — raw error over IPC
catch (error: any) {
  return { error: error.stack };
}

// ✅ GOOD — sanitized error over IPC
catch (error: any) {
  return { error: getIpcErrorMessage(error) };
}
```

### Shell & External URLs

- Never pass user-controlled strings directly to `shell.openExternal` — validate URLs first
- Only allow `https:` and `http:` protocols for external URLs
- Use `shell.openPath` for local file paths instead of `shell.openExternal`

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — unvalidated URL
shell.openExternal(userProvidedUrl);

// ✅ GOOD — validated URL
const url = new URL(userProvidedUrl);
if (url.protocol === 'https:' || url.protocol === 'http:') {
  shell.openExternal(url.href);
}
```

### File System & Child Processes

- Sanitize and validate all file paths before passing to `child_process.exec` / `spawn` / `execFile`
- Use `execFile` with explicit argument arrays instead of `exec` with string interpolation whenever possible
- Never construct shell commands by concatenating user input — always use argument arrays
- Validate that file paths stay within expected directories (e.g., within the user's repository)

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — string interpolation in shell command
exec(`git log ${userBranch}`);

// ✅ GOOD — argument array
execFile('git', ['log', userBranch]);
```

## Error Handling

- Error responses to the renderer must never include raw `error.message`, `error.stack`, or internal system details
- Log detailed errors on the backend with `electron-log`; surface only user-actionable messages to the frontend
- Do not expose internal file paths, system usernames, or environment variable names in user-facing error messages

## Updater Security

- Auto-update feeds must use HTTPS URLs
- Never disable signature verification for updates in production builds
- Validate update metadata before applying
